{{author's notes:

this is the first story in what i'm calling the "Liar's Heraldry Triptych." all of these stories are bad, but they're bad in such interesting ways. i can see how hard i was trying. i *needed* to prove to myself that i was good enough. and if i'm being absolutely fair, some of the time i managed to do an okay job. not "of quality," ever, but "okay," given the circumstances

this first entry in the trilogy showcases an approach to storytelling that i would eventually reuse in "I Stirred the Ashes," where i appropriate and reconfigure anecdotes from my life and fit them into and around a magical realism road trip (i also wrote a "Welcome to Nightvale" fanfic that uses this style {i should upload that!}). i used stories like this to help me process my life for a long time, but i never actually got any good at writing them. i couldn't muster the bravery to be completely honest with myself, and that kept me from doing my best

honestly, this one is my favorite. it's just so fucking bananas. pretentious as *hell*, to a degree that reaches *so high* that it even manages to briefly pull it off. and then the rest of the time it's excruciating, haha. and *so* disjointed. the anecodtes all pull through a consistent theme, but the way i take the reader into and out from each is just--what?

and again, it's extremely dishonest--in a very specific way that I had to keep secret even from myself. nestled in amongst many mostly-true anecdotes, the scene where i depict my relationship with my ex-wife is a complete fabrication

i understand now that this story captures my feelings and experiences as a creature of desire wasting their life on a person who is a void, and overall, i'm fascinated by the tools I used in expressing myself. i couldn't talk about the truth of my feelings directly, so i dressed a lie in symbolic poetry that pointed toward the truth}}

x
